    not_duplicate_of: []  # df1a
    pr_ref:  # df1a
    description: "**Found via self-analysis dogfooding on 2026-05-16 (round 12).**\n\n**Symptom.** Two parts of hypergumbo report **different test-coverage percentages on the same input data**, with no documented distinction or cross-reference.\n\n`hypergumbo test-coverage --input <baseline>` reports:\n\n```\nTest Coverage Estimate\n======================\nTotal functions: 4016\nTested: 2336 (58.2%)\nUntested: 1680\nTotal test functions: 19529\n```\n\n`hypergumbo sketch -t 8000` produces a sketch.md that says:\n\n```markdown\n## Tests\n\n515 test files · hypothesis, pytest, unittest\n\n*~92% estimated coverage (3705/4016 functions called by tests)*\n```\n\nSame baseline, same `4016` denominator, **two numerators** (2336 vs 3705) → **34-percentage-point discrepancy** (58.2% vs 92%).\n\n**Why this is a problem.** Neither output explains its methodology. An agent reading either number infers \"this is how thoroughly the code is tested.\" The two numbers contradict each other, and an agent that consumes both will either:\n- Trust the sketch (overstates coverage by ~34 points)\n- Trust the subcommand (understates coverage by ~34 points)\n- Notice the contradiction and waste time investigating which is right (this is what I did)\n\nIn all three cases the user loses.\n\n**Likely root cause (hypothesis).** The two metrics measure different things:\n\n- **Subcommand \"Tested: 2336\"**: probably counts functions where at least one DIRECT inbound `calls` edge originates from a test file (or some narrower definition like \"called from a test function\" not \"called transitively from any test code\").\n- **Sketch \"3705 functions called by tests\"**: probably counts functions transitively reachable from test code — which is a much wider net (includes all helper-of-helper chains).\n\nThe 3705/2336 ratio (1.59x) suggests the sketch counts ~60% more \"tested\" functions because it follows the call graph transitively, whereas the subcommand stops at direct callers.\n\nIf this hypothesis is right, both numbers are \"honest\" but they're answering different questions:\n- \"What fraction of functions have a direct test?\" → subcommand's 58%\n- \"What fraction of functions are reachable from any test?\" → sketch's 92%\n\nEither could be reasonable, but consumers can't tell them apart from the labels.\n\n**Severity.** Medium. Two contradicting numbers in the same tool's outputs on the same input. The sketch is the primary consumer-facing artifact (\"Quick start: hypergumbo .\") — its number is what most agents/humans will see. Misleading on the high side.\n\n**Fix proposal.**\n\n1. **Pick the canonical definition** and use it consistently across subcommand and sketch. Recommended: the direct-callers definition (subcommand's 58.2%) because it's the more honest answer to \"is this code tested?\" The transitive-reachability number is less actionable (a function reachable through 5 helpers from one test isn't actually exercised).\n\n2. **Or expose both with clear labels.** The sketch line could say:\n   > \"~58% directly tested (2336/4016 functions called by tests), ~92% reachable from tests\"\n   \n   And the subcommand could mention the transitive number for completeness.\n\n3. **At minimum, document the methodology.** A `--methodology` flag on test-coverage and a footnote in the sketch describing what's being counted.\n\nRecommended: (1) + (3) — pick one definition, document it everywhere.\n\n**Test gap.** Add a snapshot test asserting that `test-coverage` subcommand and `sketch` output agree on the coverage percentage to within 1% on the fixture corpus. Currently they disagree by 34 points.\n\n**Cross-references.**\n\n- `packages/hypergumbo-core/src/hypergumbo_core/cli.py` — `cmd_test_coverage` and the sketch-side coverage calc\n- `packages/hypergumbo-core/src/hypergumbo_core/sketch.py` — sketch test-coverage stringification\n- `WI-vuton-...` (filed round 11) — sibling: dead-code-maybe FPs (call-graph completeness, same upstream issue affecting BOTH numbers but in different directions)\n- `INV-mofav-...` (filed round 10) — nested-function blindness; affects both numerators because nested-helper call chains are partly invisible"  # df1a
    fields:  # df1a
      statement: "All hypergumbo outputs that report 'test coverage %' on the same input data must use the same methodology and produce the same number. Different metrics may exist, but they must use distinct labels."  # df1a
      root_cause: "test-coverage subcommand and sketch use different counting methodologies. Subcommand likely counts functions with direct inbound test->src call edges (2336/4016 = 58.2%). Sketch likely counts functions transitively reachable from any test code (3705/4016 = 92%). Same field name ('coverage'/'tested'), same denominator, different numerator. Neither output documents its methodology."  # df1a
